## Overview
#c1[overview,i5] Ky is a tiny, dependency-free HTTP client built on the Fetch API that supports modern browsers, Node.js, Bun, and Deno with comprehensive error handling and request lifecycle management.
  > readme.md:12-19 #21d1bb51a563
  > readme.md::KyError #b0aafb673926
  > readme.md::"Node.js support" #ab07ac13a258
#c2[overview,i5] The request lifecycle is managed through synchronous init hooks and asynchronous hooks like beforeRequest, beforeRetry, afterResponse, and beforeError, allowing request modifications and custom retry behaviors.
  > readme.md:473-474 #0afdd0e5e115
  > readme.md::hooks.init #6923bdd6675e
  > readme.md::"How do I implement token refresh on 401 responses?" #40ee3a9c0115
  > readme.md::"How do I stop retrying early?" #efee2ee86088
#c3[overview,i5] Ky handles retries with configurable limits, exponential backoff, response header timing, and body buffering, while supporting custom forced retries and early silent cancellations.
  > readme.md:263-274 #8e549c6b04b3
  > readme.md::"Streaming request bodies" #b8a8f8bdc942
  > readme.md::"How do I implement token refresh on 401 responses?" #40ee3a9c0115
  > readme.md::"How do I retry based on the response body?" #9e0ddb131317
  > readme.md::"How do I stop retrying early?" #efee2ee86088
  > source/core/retry-timing.ts::getRetryTimingHeader #2fe593155de0
#c4[overview,i5] Options are thoroughly validated, normalized, and merged—shallowly for context and deeply via ky.extend()—while preserving standard URL resolution and request method conventions.
  > readme.md::context #5934ac5079b7
  > readme.md:1076 #8063d124b1f4
  > readme.md::"What's the difference between `baseUrl` and `prefix`?" #944b32dbb6ef
  > source/types/options.ts::Options #be71a7a14b17
  > source/utils/normalize.ts::normalizeRetryOptions #d4a11007d970
#c5[overview,i5] A robust error hierarchy anchored by KyError provides specific errors for HTTP status failures, timeouts, network issues, response size limits, and forced retries, while intentionally excluding user-defined schema validation errors.
  > readme.md::KyError #b0aafb673926
  > source/errors/HTTPError.ts::HTTPError #b030e5d4d0e3
  > source/errors/NetworkError.ts::NetworkError #a8dd018d0a16
  > source/errors/ResponseSizeError.ts::ResponseSizeError #feb7e917cdea
  > source/errors/SchemaValidationError.ts:4 #c903980f0fa5
  > source/errors/TimeoutError.ts::TimeoutError #6a03692d6c6a
#c6[overview,i5] Cross-realm compatibility helpers ensure reliable type guarding, error classification, and feature detection for streaming requests and network error signatures across different runtime environments.
  > source/core/constants.ts::supportsRequestStreams #d420f681268a
  > source/utils/is-network-error.ts::errorMessages #b8be9b6d19c6
  > source/utils/is.ts::isError #ff175976b87b
  > source/utils/is.ts::isRequest #cc1cbebd59d3
  > source/utils/is.ts::isResponse #3283977cc352
  > source/utils/type-guards.ts::isErrorType #2f2aed5326e2
#c7[overview,i5] Timeout management enforces per-attempt limits using race conditions, abort signals, and cleanup logic that safely cancels streams and clears timers on completion or failure.
  > readme.md::timeout #140c6b988503
  > source/core/Ky.ts:1254-1258 #714583a5f3f3
  > source/utils/delay.ts::delay #446c2a0ec5f0
  > source/utils/timeout.ts::timeout #253b066c01c5

## (root)
#c8[i5] Ky is a tiny, dependency-free HTTP client based on the Fetch API that targets modern browsers, Node.js, Bun, and Deno.
  > readme.md:12-19 #21d1bb51a563
#c9[i4] Ky treats non-2xx status codes as errors after redirects and throws an HTTPError when response status is outside 200...299 range.
  > readme.md::"ky(input, options?)" #1c56f829d259
#c10[i4] The default retry limit is 2, with specific defaults for methods, status codes, backoff limit, and an exponential delay formula.
  > readme.md:263-274 #8e549c6b04b3
#c11[i3] The default per-attempt timeout is 10000 milliseconds (10 seconds), and can be configured as a number or false.
  > readme.md::timeout #140c6b988503
#c12[i3] Max response size defaults to Infinity and counts bytes from the response stream after decompression, throwing a ResponseSizeError if exceeded.
  > readme.md::maxResponseSize #01f38f174727
#c13[i4] Hooks allow modifications during the request lifecycle and include init, beforeRequest, beforeRetry, beforeError, and afterResponse.
  > readme.md:473-474 #0afdd0e5e115
#c14[i3] Unlike other hooks, init hooks are synchronous, and an error thrown by one rejects the returned promise without invoking beforeError hooks.
  > readme.md::hooks.init #6923bdd6675e
#c15[i3] Context merging is shallow; top-level properties are merged while nested objects are replaced, and only enumerable properties are copied.
  > readme.md::context #5934ac5079b7
#c16[i4] ky.extend() deep-merges options by default, appending hooks, merging headers, and accumulating search parameters.
  > readme.md:1076 #8063d124b1f4
#c17[i5] KyError is the base class for all Ky-specific errors including HTTPError, NetworkError, TimeoutError, ResponseSizeError, and ForceRetryError.
  > readme.md::KyError #b0aafb673926
#c18[i4] SchemaValidationError is intentionally not considered a Ky error and does not extend KyError because it represents a user schema rejection rather than an HTTP lifecycle failure.
  > readme.md::KyError #b0aafb673926
#c19[i4] Node.js 24.5+ supports automatic proxy configuration via environment variables when NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1 is set or the --use-env-proxy CLI flag is used.
  > readme.md::"Native proxy support" #d420abf0c034
#c20[i3] When retries are enabled, Ky buffers the entire streaming body in memory to support replaying it, which can be skipped by setting retry: {limit: 0}.
  > readme.md::"Streaming request bodies" #b8a8f8bdc942
#c21[i5] baseUrl follows standard URL resolution rules like new URL(input, baseUrl), whereas prefix does plain string joining before URL resolution and strips leading slashes from the input.
  > readme.md::"What's the difference between `baseUrl` and `prefix`?" #944b32dbb6ef
#c22[i4] Token refresh on 401 responses can be implemented by configuring `retry` for 401 status codes and using the `beforeRetry` hook to update the Authorization header.
  > readme.md::"How do I implement token refresh on 401 responses?" #40ee3a9c0115
#c23[i4] To force a retry based on the response body even with a 2xx status code, use `ky.retry()` inside an `afterResponse` hook.
  > readme.md::"How do I retry based on the response body?" #9e0ddb131317
#c24[i4] Stopping a retry early and silently can be done by returning `ky.stop` from a `beforeRetry` hook, causing the request to resolve with `undefined`.
  > readme.md::"How do I stop retrying early?" #efee2ee86088
#c25[i3] To filter HTTP errors by status, a function can be passed to `throwHttpErrors`, such as `status => status >= 500` to only throw on 5xx errors.
  > readme.md::"How do I only throw on certain HTTP errors?" #ceb9846ac62a
#c26[i3] Ky requires Node.js version 22 and later.
  > readme.md::"Node.js support" #ab07ac13a258

## source
#c27[i5] Errors occurring during request setup, such as invalid options or a throwing init hook, cause the returned promise to reject rather than throwing synchronously.
  > source/index.ts::createRequest #07856b139ac8
#c28[i4] When request setup fails, supported response types are attached to the rejected promise returning the same promise to prevent unhandled rejections when body methods are called.
  > source/index.ts::createRequest.of #664943d55fd7

